From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:52:45 +0300
Subject: Game controllers: a fixed built-in scheme next to the keyboard

Controller N (the N-th one connected, N = 1..4) plays local player N, with no
binding by hand, and the keyboard keeps working at the same time. The pads
are not in the keymap, so nothing is written to the user's configuration.

The buttons come from SDL2's game controller API, which names them by their
place on the pad (a DualShock 3 is "PS3 Controller", whatever its raw button
numbers; SDL 1.2's joystick API only has the raw numbers). The game is an SDL
1.2 program running on sdl12-compat, whose SDL2 library is already loaded, so
the few SDL2 functions needed are looked up with dlopen/dlsym at the first
use. Without SDL2 the controllers are just not there. Hot-plug: every second
the connected controllers are rescanned; a new one takes the first free
player, an unplugged one lets go of what it held and the others keep their
player.

  D-pad left/right, L1/R1   turn left / right (one turn per press)
  R2 or Cross               brake (held)
  right stick left/right    glance left / right
  Triangle                  switch camera
  Select                    score table (held)
  Start                     the game menu

In a menu the D-pad moves and changes, Cross selects, Circle and Start go
back. The controllers' state is turned into key events at the input queue
(su_GetSDLInput), so the order with the keyboard's events and the recording
stay as they were: in a menu the keys are the arrow keys, Return and Escape,
in the game keysyms beyond the keyboard's, which su_HandleEvent hands to the
player's actions (uGamepad.cpp has the table). Triggers and the stick are
digital above a dead zone.

Settings > Miscellaneous > Game Controllers shows the scheme as a read-only
menu.

